#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a commit made while HEAD is on the protected branch.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Twice in this repository a commit was written onto `main`
# because a session-start status line naming the working branch was TRUSTED at
# write time instead of re-derived. The second instance rewrote 73eff8f, the
# squashed merge of #33, with `git commit --amend`. Nothing reached the remote
# and origin/main was never touched -- but the thing that would have stopped
# the push is the ruleset's non_fast_forward rule, not any local discipline,
# and a control that only catches the mistake at push time lets the local
# history get rewritten first. This refuses it at the moment it is made.
#
# ITS FALSE-POSITIVE RATE, VERIFIED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. The claim is that no
# commit here is ever INTENDED to land directly on main, so refusing all of
# them costs nothing. That is true of current practice and is not true of this
# repository's whole history: of 80 commits on main, 34 end in `(#N)` and 46 do
# not. The 46 are all dated 2026-09-04 or earlier -- before the ruleset existed
# -- and every commit from 2026-09-06 onward arrived as a squash merge. So the
# honest statement is that the false-positive rate is zero against the practice
# in force since the ruleset, measured over the 34 merges since, and that the
# practice changed once and could change again.
#
# THE ESCAPE IS DELIBERATELY GIT'S OWN. `git commit --no-verify` skips this,
# and no repository-specific override is added on top. A second escape would be
# a second thing to remember and would not be more honest than the standard one
# every git user already knows.
set -euo pipefail

protected="main"
branch="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo "")"

if [ "$branch" != "$protected" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<EOM
REFUSED: you are committing on '$protected'.

Landing on '$protected' is enforced by a GitHub ruleset, not by convention:
a pull request is required, three status checks must pass, bypass_actors is
empty, and non_fast_forward is forbidden. A direct push is rejected with
GH013. So a commit made here cannot become part of '$protected' by any route
-- it can only sit in local history, where the danger is that it lands ON TOP
of a squashed merge and an --amend then rewrites it.

That has happened twice in this repository, both times from the same cause:
a status line naming the working branch was trusted at write time instead of
re-derived.

WHAT TO DO:

  git switch -c <branch-name>     # then commit; the work is unchanged
  git stash                       # if you would rather move it later

The staged changes are untouched either way -- nothing has been lost.

If you genuinely mean to commit on '$protected', use git's own escape:

  git commit --no-verify

EOM
exit 1
